Explanation:
- Exception in thread "main" – The exception occurred in the main thread.
- ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException – The program tried to access an invalid array index.
- Index 5 out of bounds for length 2 – The array has only two elements, but index 5 was requested.
- StackTraceDemo.java:4 – The exception occurred at line 4.
This exception is uncaught because the example does not contain a suitable catch block.
C. What happens if a broader catch block comes first?
Incorrect example:
try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
}
catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("General exception");
}
catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Arithmetic exception");
}


Compilation error: The second catch block is unreachable because ArithmeticException is already covered by the earlier Exception catch block.

Correct order:
try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
}
catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Arithmetic exception");
}
catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("General exception");
}


8. Joint Design Question
Should the ATM catch every exception using a single catch(Exception e)?

It is technically possible, but it is not the best approach for normal ATM transaction handling.

Advantages:
- Simple code.
- Can handle many exception types in one place.
- Useful as a final unexpected-exception handler.
Disadvantages:
- Cannot easily distinguish invalid input from insufficient balance.
- Makes debugging difficult.
- Different failures may require different recovery actions.
- May hide important programming errors.
Conclusion: Specific catch blocks are better because they allow the ATM to handle each failure correctly. A broad catch(Exception e) may be used as a final fallback after specific handlers, with proper logging and safe failure handling.
9. Alternative Concept – Without Exception Handling
Instead of using exceptions, the ATM can validate all inputs before processing the withdrawal.

The alternative approach uses:
- Scanner.hasNextLong() to check account number input.
- Scanner.hasNextDouble() to check withdrawal amount.
- if-else conditions to validate the account and balance.
- Error codes such as -1 to indicate failure.
Alternative Java code

import java.util.Scanner;

public class ATMWithoutException {

    static int withdraw(long account, double amount,
                        double balance) {

        if (account != 123456) {
            return -1;
        }

        if (amount <= 0) {
            return -2;
        }

        if (amount > balance) {
            return -3;
        }

        return 1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        double balance = 5000;

        System.out.print("Enter account number: ");

        if (!sc.hasNextLong()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid account number");
            sc.close();
            return;
        }

        long account = sc.nextLong();

        System.out.print("Enter withdrawal amount: ");

        if (!sc.hasNextDouble()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid amount");
            sc.close();
            return;
        }

        double amount = sc.nextDouble();

        int result = withdraw(account, amount, balance);

        if (result == 1) {
            balance = balance - amount;
            System.out.println("Withdrawal successful!");
            System.out.println("Remaining balance: " + balance);
        }
        else if (result == -1) {
            System.out.println("Invalid account number");
        }
        else if (result == -2) {
            System.out.println("Amount must be positive");
        }
        else if (result == -3) {
            System.out.println("Insufficient balance");
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}



