Comparison of both approaches
Feature	Exception handling	Error-code approach
Invalid input	Catch exception	Check using hasNext
Negative amount	Throw exception	Return -2
Insufficient balance	Custom exception	Return -3
Missing file	File exception	Check file existence
Readability	Structured error handling	More validation conditions
Debugging	Exception messages and stack traces	Manual error checking

Time and space complexity
Let \(d\) represent the depth of the call stack when an exception is thrown.
Operation	Exception approach	Error-code approach
Normal withdrawal	O(1)	O(1)
Invalid input validation	O(1) per check	O(1) per check
Exception creation and throwing	Typically O(d)	Not required
Extra memory	O(d) for stack-trace information	O(1)
Overall auxiliary space	O(d) during exception handling	O(1)

The actual exception cost depends on JVM implementation and stack depth. Exception creation and stack-trace generation are generally more expensive than returning an error code.

Best approach: Exception handling is suitable for exceptional situations such as missing files and unexpected failures. Error codes and input validation are suitable for expected situations such as negative amounts and insufficient balance.

